## Solution

We evaluate

$$I = \int_{0}^{\pi/2}\left(\frac{\ln(\cos x)}{\sin x}\right)^{2}dx = \int_{0}^{\pi/2}\frac{\ln^{2}(\cos x)}{\sin^{2}x}\,dx.$$

**Integrability check.** Near $x=0$: $\ln(\cos x)\sim -x^{2}/2$, so $\ln^{2}(\cos x)/\sin^{2}x \sim x^{2}/4 \to 0$. Near $x=\pi/2$, with $t=\pi/2-x\to 0^{+}$: $\cos x=\sin t\sim t$, $\sin x=\cos t\to 1$, so the integrand $\sim \ln^{2}t$, and $\int_{0}\ln^{2}t\,dt$ converges. Hence $I$ is finite.

**Integration by parts.** Set

$$u=\ln^{2}(\cos x),\qquad dv=\csc^{2}x\,dx,$$

so that

$$du = 2\ln(\cos x)\cdot\frac{-\sin x}{\cos x}\,dx = -2\ln(\cos x)\tan x\,dx,\qquad v=-\cot x.$$

Then

$$I=\Bigl[-\cot x\,\ln^{2}(\cos x)\Bigr]_{0}^{\pi/2}+\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\cot x\cdot\bigl(-2\ln(\cos x)\tan x\bigr)\,dx.$$

**Boundary terms vanish.** As $x\to\pi/2^{-}$, with $t=\pi/2-x$: $\cot x=\tan t\sim t$ and $\ln^{2}(\cos x)=\ln^{2}(\sin t)\sim \ln^{2}t$, so $t\ln^{2}t\to 0$. As $x\to 0^{+}$: $\ln(\cos x)\sim -x^{2}/2$, so $\ln^{2}(\cos x)\sim x^{4}/4$ while $\cot x\sim 1/x$, giving $\sim x^{3}/4\to 0$. Thus

$$I = -2\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\ln(\cos x)\,\underbrace{\cot x\,\tan x}_{=1}\,dx = -2\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\ln(\cos x)\,dx.$$

**Classical integral.** Using the standard result (obtained, e.g., from $f(a)=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\cos^{a}x\,dx$ and differentiating under the integral sign at $a=0$, or from the symmetry $\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\ln(\cos x)\,dx=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\ln(\sin x)\,dx$ combined with $\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\ln(\sin 2x)\,dx$):

$$\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\ln(\cos x)\,dx = -\frac{\pi}{2}\ln 2.$$

**Conclusion.**

$$I = -2\cdot\left(-\frac{\pi}{2}\ln 2\right) = \pi\ln 2.$$

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\left(\frac{\ln(\cos x)}{\sin x}\right)^{2}dx = \pi\ln 2.}$$

